{"file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/finite_spectral_shadows.tex","new_string":"A single odd integer $x_0$ produces one deterministic valuation word\n$w(x_0)=(b_0,b_1,\\dots)$, $b_i=v_2(3x_i+1)\\ge1$, with $x_{i+1}=S(x_i)$. (For\nbackground on the $3x+1$ problem and its stochastic models see the survey and\ncollection of Lagarias~\\cite{lagarias1985,lagarias2010} and the annealed\nanalyses of Sinai, Kontorovich--Lagarias, and\nTao~\\cite{sinai2003,kontorovichlagarias,tao2022}.) The\nannealed law is the pushforward of Haar measure on $\\Z_2$ under\n$x\\mapsto v_2(3x+1)$, i.e.\\ $\\Pr(b)=2^{-b}$.","old_string":"A single odd integer $x_0$ produces one deterministic valuation word\n$w(x_0)=(b_0,b_1,\\dots)$, $b_i=v_2(3x_i+1)\\ge1$, with $x_{i+1}=S(x_i)$. The\nannealed law is the pushforward of Haar measure on $\\Z_2$ under\n$x\\mapsto v_2(3x+1)$, i.e.\\ $\\Pr(b)=2^{-b}$.","replace_all":false}